Title: Diagnosis

Details: The test `skipUsingRead()` fails because it wraps an InputStream with an overridden skip(long s) method that always returns 0. When `IOUtils.skip(InputStream, long)` is called on this wrapped input, the implementation tries to skip bytes by repeatedly calling `input.skip(numToSkip)`. It relies on the behavior defined in the Javadoc comment mentioned in the bug report, which says that skip() on InputStreams will only return 0 if the end of stream (EOF) has been reached.

However, in this case, the skip method on the wrapped InputStream returns 0 immediately without actually reaching EOF. This causes the `IOUtils.skip` method's loop to break early, thus skipping fewer bytes than requested, contrary to the method's contract.

Specifically, the core issue is in the code snippet of `IOUtils.skip` method:

```java
 96 : while (numToSkip > 0) {
 97 :     long skipped = input.skip(numToSkip);
 98 :     if (skipped == 0) {
 99 :         break;
100 :     }
101 :     numToSkip -= skipped;
102 : }
```

Here, if `skip()` returns 0, the method concludes EOF or no more progress possible and breaks the loop, returning the number of bytes successfully skipped so far.

The contract mentioned in the Javadoc "This method will only skip less than the requested number of bytes if the end of the input stream has been reached." does not hold in cases where the underlying InputStream's skip() method returns 0 even though EOF is not reached. For example, filter streams that do not support skipping at all or have overridden `skip()` to always return 0.

Thus, the root cause of the bug is that the `IOUtils.skip` method is not robust against `skip()` implementations that return 0 without EOF. It is too trusting of the InputStream.skip() contract and fails early instead of trying alternative ways to skip or detect EOF.

Possible fixes include changing the method to fallback on reading and discarding bytes when the skip returns 0, or to retry multiple times, or different techniques to advance the stream pointer in such cases.

Recent bug reports are unrelated to this issue, focused on compression dictionary sizes or compression algorithms, so they do not add useful insight.

Overall, the failing test case simulates this exact problematic condition by wrapping the InputStream and making skip always return 0, thereby exposing the incorrect behavior in IOUtils.skip.

Hence the `IOUtils.skip(InputStream, long)` method needs to be fixed to honor its contract and handle zero skip returns correctly.

